# WebDesk (Zone B) — Caddy proxy. WSK-01 stub, hardened at WSK-02.
#
# This is the ONLY public listener in the Zone B topology (design §02/§03).
#
# WSK-02 CHANGE — the stub used to `reverse_proxy /admin* payload:3000` on this public vhost.
# That contradicted WSK-D20 and design D-5 ("Payload admin is never public") and would have
# exposed the admin panel the moment the stack ran anywhere reachable. Removed.
#
# The invariant this file must preserve, from WSK-D20:
#   /v1  is the ONLY contract a client site ever sees.
#   Payload's admin, its GraphQL, and its unscoped/raw collection REST are NEVER routed here.
#
# WSK-48b CHANGE — the second layer is GONE, and this says so honestly. Until Payload was deleted
# (WSK-D37), `payload/src/public-gateway.mjs` carried its own hardcoded denylist (/admin,
# /api/graphql, /api/*) behind this proxy, proven by `payload/test/lockdown.test.mjs`. Neither
# exists any more. What stands in its place:
#   * there is nothing left to leak: no Payload admin, GraphQL or raw collection REST process
#     runs anywhere in this stack (the compose `payload` service and its port are deleted), so a
#     mis-edited route here can no longer expose them, only the api;
#   * this file is an ALLOWLIST by construction: /healthz, /status/*, /control/*, /v1/*, /forms/*
#     and /media/* are the only routes, everything else falls into the final `handle` and is a 404,
#     and the explicit /admin* and /api/* denials below are kept so a future service cannot start
#     answering those paths by accident;
#   * the api authenticates and authorises behind it (API key + scope, PublicTenantGuard, the
#     control channel's mTLS + Keycloak + WS4) and every tenant query runs under FORCE RLS.
# The proxy is therefore the first gate and the api + the database are the rest; there is no longer
# an independent denylist process in between. What pins the routing is `check-compose-ports.mjs`
# (nothing but this proxy is published) and the api's own suites (webdesk/api/test/delivery/,
# the WSK-04 cross-path suite).
#
# WSK-28 CHANGE — two additions, both from the ops baseline:
#   1. /status/* — the public status page (webdesk/ops/, generated by status-page service into
#      the shared webdesk_status volume, served here directly via file_server — see
#      infra/runbooks/webdesk-zoneb-status-page.md). Does not touch any Zone A data (Zone B
#      cannot read Zone A telemetry — this page reports Zone B's own local health only).
#   2. CDN-bypass enforcement on /media/* (design §11a: "a media path that bypasses the CDN is a
#      defect") — Cloudflare is expected to inject a shared-secret header
#      (X-Webdesk-Edge-Verify) on every request it forwards to this origin, via an Origin/
#      Transform Rule configured IN Cloudflare (not in this repo — Zone B holds no Cloudflare API
#      credential beyond the purge-scoped token, design §03 egress table). A request missing (or
#      mismatching) that header reached this origin directly, bypassing Cloudflare, and is
#      refused BEFORE the media route is reached. See webdesk/ops/scripts/check-cdn-bypass.mjs
#      for the auditor that proves this check-logic would fail if the direction were reversed
#      (i.e. would catch a config that serves media with the header absent). Empty
#      WEBDESK_EDGE_VERIFY_SECRET (unset, dev default) is a documented DEV-ONLY escape hatch —
#      see the env var's own comment in .env.example; a box behind a real Cloudflare zone MUST
#      set this before going live, or the enforcement below is checking a request against an
#      empty string and passing everything.
{
	auto_https off
}

:80 {
	# =========================================================================================
	# EVERY ROUTE IS INSIDE A `handle` BLOCK, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
	# =========================================================================================
	# This file previously used bare `respond` + `reverse_proxy` directives side by side. Caddy
	# does NOT execute Caddyfile directives in written order — it sorts them by its own directive
	# order, and `respond` sorts BEFORE `reverse_proxy`. The final catch-all `respond "webdesk"
	# 404` has no matcher, so it matched every request and terminated it before any proxy ran.
	#
	# Net effect, found 2026-08-27 by actually booting the stack: /v1, /forms and /media were
	# NEVER routed. The public content surface did not work at all. `caddy validate` passed,
	# `caddy adapt` showed the bare static_response ordered ahead of all three reverse_proxy
	# handlers, and — the reason this survived — every SECURITY probe still passed. /admin,
	# /api/graphql, /api/users/login and /control/* all returned 404, because nothing was routed
	# anywhere. A perfect-looking lockdown produced by a completely broken proxy.
	#
	# `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive and evaluated by matcher specificity, so ordering
	# becomes explicit and local instead of implicit and global. Do not reintroduce a bare
	# `respond` or `reverse_proxy` at this level.
	#
	# The invariant this file must preserve (WSK-D20 / design D-5):
	#   /v1 is the ONLY content contract a client site ever sees.
	#   Payload's admin, its GraphQL, and its unscoped/raw collection REST are NEVER routed here.

	# --- health -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	handle /healthz {
		respond "webdesk proxy ok" 200
	}

	# --- public status page (WSK-28) ----------------------------------------------------------
	# Own service health only. Never a Zone A view (see infra/runbooks/webdesk-zoneb-otel.md §1 —
	# same boundary, opposite direction: Zone B cannot show what it cannot read). Serves an empty
	# volume (404) until the status-page service is deployed.
	handle_path /status/* {
		root * /srv/status
		file_server
	}

	# --- EXPLICIT DENIALS — declared BEFORE the proxied surface, and enforced by being their own
	# --- `handle` blocks rather than relying on directive order (WSK-D20 / D-5) ----------------
	#   /admin*            -> was Payload's admin (internal listener only); the app is deleted (WSK-48b), the
	#                         denial stays so nothing can start answering it by accident
	#   /api/graphql*      -> was Payload's GraphQL; covered by the /api/* denial
	#   /api/*  (raw REST) -> was Payload's unscoped collection REST, which bypassed the /v1 contract
	#   /control/*         -> the control plane: mTLS + Keycloak + WS4 (WSK-21/22), never public
	handle /admin* {
		respond 404
	}
	handle /api/* {
		respond 404
	}
	# /control/* (WSK-63a, design write-plane �11): 404 for everyone EXCEPT a request that carries the
	# gate header the host-nginx control vhost (`control.webdesk.internal`, ops/nginx/) adds after it has
	# verified the client certificate. The gate is a shared secret between that vhost and this file, the
	# same pattern as X-Webdesk-Edge-Verify below, with one deliberate difference: it FAILS CLOSED. An
	# unset WEBDESK_CONTROL_GATE_SECRET means NO request matches the gate, so /control/* stays 404.
	# (The edge-verify matcher is fail-open for dev; a control plane must not be.)
	# The public api.gaiada.online nginx vhost also strips this header, so an outside caller cannot
	# present it even if it guessed the value. Layer 1 (the app re-verifying the forwarded cert) and
	# Layer 2 (the Keycloak token) still decide authentication; the gate only proves "this request
	# came through the vhost that did the TLS client-cert check".
	# Local order inside a `handle` is what you want: the matched `respond` answers first.
	handle /control/* {
		@no_control_gate expression `"{$WEBDESK_CONTROL_GATE_SECRET}" == "" || {http.request.header.X-Webdesk-Control-Gate} != "{$WEBDESK_CONTROL_GATE_SECRET}"`
		respond @no_control_gate 404
		reverse_proxy api:3000 {
			# The api needs the verified cert header, not the gate secret.
			header_up -X-Webdesk-Control-Gate
		}
	}

	# --- PUBLIC content surface ---------------------------------------------------------------
	# WSK-63a: every public route below strips the two control-channel headers before the api sees them.
	# Layer 1 reads x-webdesk-mtls-cert-pem; a public caller must never be able to supply it.
	# The frozen /v1 envelope (WSK-06). WSK-48a rehomed it: it is served by the api's `delivery`
	# module (webdesk/api/src/delivery/), no longer by the Payload app's public gateway (that process
	# was killed on the live box on 2026-09-09 and /v1 was down until this move; Payload is removed
	# per WSK-D37). Note /v1/t/:tenantSlug/forms/* and /v1/t/:tenantSlug/media/* are ALSO api routes
	# (WSK-10 / WSK-07) and are now reachable through this one rule — both are public-by-design
	# surfaces whose own guards (API key + scope, PublicTenantGuard) decide; nothing under /internal/*
	# or /control/* is routed here.
	handle /v1/* {
		reverse_proxy api:3000 {
			header_up -X-Webdesk-Control-Gate
			header_up -X-Webdesk-Mtls-Cert-Pem
		}
	}

	# Public form submission (WSK-10). Scoped per-tenant by API key; never the control plane.
	handle /forms/* {
		reverse_proxy api:3000 {
			header_up -X-Webdesk-Control-Gate
			header_up -X-Webdesk-Mtls-Cert-Pem
		}
	}

	# Public media (WSK-07), behind the CDN-bypass gate.
	handle /media/* {
		# A request whose X-Webdesk-Edge-Verify header does not match the shared secret Cloudflare
		# injects did not come through Cloudflare — refuse it before it reaches the api service.
		# The leading `!= ""` clause is the dev fail-soft escape hatch: unset (dev default, no CDN
		# in front) makes the matcher false and nothing is blocked, so WSK-07's dev media tests are
		# unaffected. That also makes it fail-OPEN in production, which is why
		# `ops/scripts/check-cdn-bypass.mjs --assert-configured` exists — run it on the box.
		#
		# Inside a `handle` block the local order IS what you want: the matched `respond` answers
		# first, and anything not matched falls through to the proxy below.
		@missing_edge_verify expression `"{$WEBDESK_EDGE_VERIFY_SECRET}" != "" && {http.request.header.X-Webdesk-Edge-Verify} != "{$WEBDESK_EDGE_VERIFY_SECRET}"`
		respond @missing_edge_verify 403 {
			body "direct origin access is not permitted — this path is served through the CDN only"
		}
		reverse_proxy api:3000 {
			header_up -X-Webdesk-Control-Gate
			header_up -X-Webdesk-Mtls-Cert-Pem
		}
	}

	# --- everything else ----------------------------------------------------------------------
	handle {
		respond "webdesk" 404
	}
}
